 "draft": "## Latest JOLTS Report: Labor Market Assessment (through August 2026)\n\n**Bottom line:** The U.S. labor market shows moderate but cooling demand. Job openings remain historically decent but have pulled back from recent highs, while the quits rate \u2014 a key barometer of worker confidence \u2014 sits at a subdued level, signaling that workers are less willing to voluntarily leave jobs than in the post-pandemic boom.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcca Chart\n**File path:** `/Users/dakshinsiva/economic-analysis/outputs/jolts-job-openings-quits-rate-2019-prese-2026-10-13.png`\n\n---\n\n### Key Evidence Bullets\n\n- **Job openings** in the most recent reading stand at **7,079,000** (JTSJOL, 2026-08-01), down from a 12-month peak of **7,585,000** in April 2026 (JTSJOL, 2026-04-01) \u2014 a decline of ~506,000 openings in four months.\n- **Quits rate** is at **1.90%** (JTSQUR, 2026-08-01), near its 12-month low, having briefly touched **2.00%** at multiple points throughout the period (JTSQUR, 2025-11-01 through 2026-06-01). This suggests workers feel *less confident* about voluntarily leaving jobs.\n- **Hiring rate** in August was **3.30%** (JTSHIR, 2026-08-01), broadly stable within a narrow range of **3.10%\u20133.50%** over the past 12 months \u2014 indicating employers are still bringing workers on, but without acceleration.\n- **Layoffs and discharges** fell to **1,641,000** in August (JTSLDL, 2026-08-01), the lowest in the 12-month window, suggesting employers are *retaining* workers even as they pull back on new openings.\n- The gap between openings (~7.1M) and layoffs (~1.6M) remains wide (JTSJOL & JTSLDL, 2026-08-01), confirming the labor market is not in outright contraction.\n\n---\n\n### Caveats\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Release lags:** JOLTS data is published with approximately a one-month lag and is subject to revision in subsequent releases.\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Revisions:** Prior months' figures (especially the April 2026 peak of 7,585,000) may be revised up or down.\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Context not retrieved:** Pre-2025 historical peaks (e.g., the ~12M openings in 2022) were not in the evidence window and are not quoted here per methodology rules.",
